<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Livius Andronicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 284–c. 204 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Lucius Livius Andronicus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Livius Andronicus was a Greek from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Tarentum</a> who became the first known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet and dramatist. Brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> probably as a captive, he translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> into Latin verse, and in 240 BCE he staged the first Latin play adapted from a Greek original. His work began the Roman tradition of literature modelled on Greek forms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livius-andronicus.jpg" alt="Livius Andronicus" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PGS 1984 - information from the source may be found on the original file. I did the manual job - (there: extracting t... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Livius_Andronicus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
